  "slug": "stress-multiomics-ade6",
  "summary": "Recent items couple transcriptomic, metabolomic, and proteomic profiling to functional tests of stress regulators in model and crop plants.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is integrated omics, most often transcriptomics paired with metabolomics or proteomics, applied to plant stress responses. Maize seedlings under PEG osmotic stress were profiled with RNA-seq, widely targeted metabolomics, and phytohormone profiling, identifying ZmPP2CA11 as an ABA-linked negative regulator. A heat stress memory dataset in Arabidopsis provides transcriptome, metabolome, and proteome layers across memory-phase intervals, while blueberry postharvest changes were resolved tissue-specifically with DIABLO, MOFA2, and network analysis. Reviews on melatonin metabolism, RNA m6A modification, safflower omics, olive biotic stress, and ABA stomatal biology frame these efforts. The newer element is coupling omics layers to functional dissection, as with SGD1 regulating flowering time in Setaria and GhMYBL repressing GhXTH22 and GhALP1 in cotton fiber elongation. Depth varies across items, and several are reviews or dataset descriptions rather than validated mechanisms.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-22"
}
